00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:40.880
There is also a new method called Thought Retriever. Thought Retriever is a model agnostic approach that

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:46.240
retrieves and reuses in LLM's own filtered intermediate thoughts from past queries instead of raw

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:51.580
data chunks. This bypasses context-length limits while maintaining a self-evolving long-term

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:56.640
memory of useful thoughts. The memory grows more capable with continued use. A new benchmark called

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:01.920
academic-eval tests, faithful use of ultra-long academic paper contexts. The method showed at

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:08.800
least 7.6% F1 and 16% winrate gains over baselines. Now, let's pop the hood on something interesting

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:14.060
that ties a lot of these reasoning improvements together. Everyone talks about best-of-end selection

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:19.040
as if majority voting or simple probability averaging magically surfaces the best answer.

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:24.280
In practice, radial consensus score reveals that semantic geometry carries far more signal than

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:30.180
discrete counts or raw logits. The core insight is to embed candidate answers, compute their weighted

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:36.160
fresh A mean as the geometric center of mass, then rank each answer by its radial Euclidean distance to

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:41.960
that center. This turns consensus detection into a continuous geometric operation rather than a

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:47.720
combinatorial vote. Different waiting schemes, whether uniform frequency-based or model probability,

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:52.960
plug directly into the same framework without retraining. The efficiency comes from operating

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:59.140
an embedding space rather than token space. Radial consensus score adds negligible overhead yet

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:04.940
consistently outperforms self-consistency and probability baselines. The gains widen as the

00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:11.400
sampling budget grows from 4 to 64 candidates. It also serves as a drop-in replacement inside multi-agent

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:16.840
debate loops. The gotcha that bites most teams is assuming all embeddings are equally calibrated.

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:22.400
Poor embedding models collapse the radial signal. The quality gain is most pronounced on long-form

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:28.380
reasoning where surface-level agreement diverges from semantic correctness. Use radial consensus score

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:34.400
when you have at least 8 to 16 samples and care about robustness over raw speed. Skip it for tiny

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:39.440
sample sizes or when your embedding model has not been tuned on your domain. In those cases,

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:46.360
fall back to probability-weighted selection. If you have not tried Gemini Robotics ER1.6 on

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:51.160
instrument reading and sequential manipulation tasks, this week is the time, especially if you

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:57.140
have access to DeepMind's Robotics APIs. The embodied reasoning improvements hit exactly where prior

00:08:57.140 --> 00:09:09.040
models fell short. Download the Quoppus 3.5-27BV3RYSGGUFQuant and run it locally in LM studio with the

